-- Runner — an endless scroller built from chunks, and the seeded generator.
--
-- What this exercises that the other examples do not:
--   * `stream`: a level composed at build time by drawing chunks at random,
--     rather than drawn by hand. Four small files become a long course.
--   * `seed`: the one number the whole course follows from. Change it and the
--     game is different; leave it and every console, every run and every trace
--     agree exactly.
--   * `random` at run time, for the coin that keeps moving ahead of the bird.
--
-- "Endless" here means "long enough, and different every seed" — which is what
-- the era's own scrollers did. A course generated as the player flew would be
-- reproducible only if every machine drew in the same order at the same tick,
-- and that is a promise not worth making when a tilemap in ROM will do.

start play

seed 20260725

scene play

-- 24 chunks of 6 cells each: a 144-cell course, laid side by side. The chunks
-- share a legend, so `#` is a pipe in all of them.
stream course from open.dmtl, lowpipe.dmtl, highpipe.dmtl, pipemid.dmtl 24 wide

camera follows bird1

-- Six cells a second, absolute, and that is the interesting choice in this file.
-- Everywhere else a speed is relative, because the playfield is the screen and a
-- rally has to take the same seconds on a wide one as on a narrow one. Here the
-- playfield is a *course*, 144 hand-drawn cells on every console, so "how fast"
-- and "how hard" are both measured in cells: at 30vw the Mega Drive's bird would
-- cover the same pipes at twice the speed and be playing a different game.
create object bird (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 6, sprite hero.svg)
create object prize (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 0, sprite coin.svg)

-- Rows 13 to 16 are the corridor every chunk leaves open: the low pipes stop
-- below it, the high ones above it, and the middle chunk's slot contains it. A
-- vocabulary of chunks has properties like that, and the game is allowed to rely
-- on them; it is why the chunks are hand-drawn and only their *order* is
-- generated.
create bird bird1 in play (x 1, y 13, xdirection 1)

create prize prize1 in play (x 12, y 14)

create text scoretag in play (x 1, y 1, text "coins:")
create number score in play (value 0, x 8, y 1)

-- Gravity and the flap. The mechanic is the platformer's, but the numbers are a
-- flapper's rather than a jumper's: a small impulse against a heavy pull, so one
-- press buys about one row and the bird is always either climbing or falling.
-- A jump-sized impulse here would send it over the pipes entirely, and a game
-- you can fly above is not a game.
when always in play then bird1.ydirection as min(bird1.ydirection + 0.09, 0.9)

control bird1 a (ydirection -1.2) on press

-- Pipes and ground are tiles, named by the chunks' shared legend.
when bird1 touches pipe, ground then scene as gameover

-- The bird flies until the end of the course.
when bird1 hits screenright then scene as won
when bird1 hits screentop then ydirection as 0

-- The coin keeps its distance ahead of the view, at a height drawn fresh each
-- time from inside the corridor. This is the one place a game needs `random`:
-- the course is fixed by the seed, but where the reward sits is decided as the
-- game runs — and, because the generator is the language's own rather than the
-- host's, decided identically on every machine and in every replay.
when prize.right < camera.x in play then (x, y) as (camera.x + screenwidth, random(13, 16))

when bird1 hits prize then (prize1.x, score.value) as (camera.x + screenwidth, score.value + 1)

-- The HUD rides the camera, label and counter alike, or it is left behind in the
-- first chunk. A scrolling scene draws its counters with sprites for the same
-- reason the caves does: the background cannot hold still while it scrolls.
when always in play then (scoretag.x, scoretag.y, score.x, score.y) as (camera.x + 1, camera.y + 1, camera.x + 8, camera.y + 1)

-- --------------------------------------------------------------- endings ----

scene gameover

create text lost in gameover (x centerx - 4, y centery, text "clipped it")

when a pressed in gameover then scene as play

scene won

create text finished in won (x centerx - 5, y centery, text "flew it all")

when a pressed in won then scene as play
